   "my_inferred_type_of_app": "Pi x0:B. (Pi x1:Type 0. B)",
   "adjudication": "MY KERNEL IS RIGHT; the reference kernel has a capture bug. Derivation from RULES_DT.md: the inner Lam lambda A:Type 0. x has body x whose type is A_outer (the middle binder); by the Lam rule its type is Pi A_inner:Type 0. A_outer. Beta-reducing the type by hand: f B = lambda x:B. lambda A:Type 0. x : Pi x:B. Pi A:Type 0. B, exactly the expected type. The reference kernel instead infers the inner Pi's codomain as A_inner: its named representation builds Pi('A', Type 0, Var('A')) where Var('A') is meant to be the outer A, and its own name-based reading captures it with the inner binder. The bug fires only when a Lam's body type mentions an outer variable whose name equals the Lam's binder name (shadowing); none of the 18 shared tests or other battery cases has that shape, which is why the suites never caught it.",
   "resolution": "Rename-on-entry fix applied to kernel_dt.py 2026-11-25 (freshen the binder before extending the context, in infer's and check's Lam rules; defeq fresh names also avoid ctx keys). NOT the proposed blind rename of the Pi binder at construction, which was shown wrong before applying: it would regress the dependent-family case. After the fix: battery capture 5/5, a2 agrees, and no other case changed anywhere.",
   "impact": "narrow: only shadowed-binder Lam types are mis-inferred. The 18/18 and 11/11 suites are unaffected."
  }
